package pages

import "saui/templates/layout"

templ Limits(csrfToken string) {
	@layout.Base("Limits", "Where SAUI works and where it does not. Latency-sensitive UIs, offline-first apps, and the honest tradeoffs you need to know.", csrfToken, "/limits", "/static/css/limits.css") {
		<section class="page-hero">
			<div class="page-hero__content">
				<h1>Limits</h1>
				<p class="lead">{ `SAUI is the wrong choice for some applications. These are genuine exceptions, not contrived ones.` }</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<article>
			<section>
				<p>{ `Credibility requires honesty. A pattern that claims to be appropriate for every
					application is not a pattern; it is marketing. SAUI has real limits and stating
					them plainly is part of the argument, not a concession to it.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>Real-time collaborative editing</h2>
				<p>{ `When multiple users concurrently mutate the same document - a shared document editor,
					a collaborative whiteboard, a live code editor - the server-authoritative model
					requires conflict resolution that SAUI does not provide out of the box.` }</p>
				<p>{ `Concurrent writes to the same state require CRDTs (Conflict-free Replicated Data
					Types) or operational transforms to merge diverging edit histories. These are
					hard problems and the SAUI append-only event log does not solve them.` }</p>
				<p>{ `For single-user editing with server persistence - a CMS, a form, a document editor
					used by one person at a time - SAUI is entirely appropriate. The limit is specifically
					real-time multi-user concurrent mutation of shared state.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>Canvas and creative tools</h2>
				<p>{ `Tools like Figma, Photoshop, or a code editor have an interaction model where local
					state is inherent. Every mouse movement is not a server round-trip. Every keypress
					does not trigger a projection update. The latency of server authorisation is
					incompatible with the performance requirement.` }</p>
				<p>{ `These applications require a local state model with selective server persistence.
					The SAUI round-trip model is not appropriate here.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>Offline-first PWAs</h2>
				<p>{ `Applications that must function without a network connection require client-side
					state with sync-on-reconnect. A service worker can cache projections, but mutations
					made offline need to be queued and replayed when connectivity is restored.` }</p>
				<p>{ `SAUI is online-first. The gateway cannot authorise actions it cannot reach.
					For applications where offline operation is a primary requirement, not a nice-to-have,
					a client-side state model with server sync is appropriate.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>Games and simulations</h2>
				<p>{ `High-frequency local state - game physics, simulation state, real-time rendering -
					is a performance requirement. Server-authoritative game state exists (it is the
					standard cheat-prevention model in multiplayer games) but the implementation is
					specialised and the tooling is not general-purpose web infrastructure.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>High-frequency financial data</h2>
				<p>{ `Live tickers and order books can be delivered via SSE or WebSocket projection
					streams, and the SAUI model applies at the architecture level. At scale, however,
					this becomes an infrastructure problem: fan-out to thousands of connections
					receiving updates at high frequency requires careful design that goes beyond
					the pattern described here.` }</p>
				<p>{ `The SAUI model is sound for this use case. The implementation demands are significant.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>The honest boundary</h2>
				<p>{ `If your application is a food ordering site, a banking dashboard, a booking system,
					a CMS, an admin panel, a SaaS product, or most of what is built for the web -
					SAUI is appropriate and the complexity of the SPA model is not justified.` }</p>
				<p>{ `If your application is a real-time collaborative editor, a creative tool, an
					offline-first mobile web app, or a high-frequency data display - evaluate carefully.
					SAUI may still apply partially, or may not be the right fit.` }</p>
				<p><a href="/cases">See the pattern applied to common domains</a></p>
			</section>
		</article>
	}
}
